WEBVTT

00:02.394 --> 00:04.673
<i>♪ It seems today
 that all you see ♪</i>

00:04.824 --> 00:07.754
<i>♪ Is violence in movies
 and sex on TV ♪</i>

00:07.923 --> 00:11.746
<i>♪ But where are those
 good old-fashioned values ♪</i>

00:11.765 --> 00:14.753
<i>♪ On which we used to rely? ♪</i>

00:14.772 --> 00:18.110
<i>♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪</i>

00:18.263 --> 00:21.359
<i>♪ Lucky there's a man who
 positively can do ♪</i>

00:21.453 --> 00:23.105
<i>♪ All the things that make us ♪</i>

00:23.123 --> 00:24.290
<i>♪ Laugh and cry ♪</i>

00:24.384 --> 00:30.229
<i>♪ He's... a...
 Fam... ily... Guy! ♪</i>

00:36.319 --> 00:39.323
It's such a treat to be
at the water park with you guys.

00:39.476 --> 00:40.660
I don't want to
freak anyone out,

00:40.812 --> 00:43.316
but Meg is just openly weeping
at the touch tank.

00:43.334 --> 00:45.579
[crying] It's been so long.

00:45.673 --> 00:48.752
-Okay. All right.
-I need this so much.

00:48.772 --> 00:50.273
-This is a gift.
-Just gonna...

00:50.425 --> 00:51.517
-You have no idea.
-...move along here.

00:51.669 --> 00:53.338
You're giving me a gift
right now.

00:53.356 --> 00:54.765
You-you take care now.

00:54.860 --> 00:58.273
Yeesh. Imagine being that
desperate for human connection.

00:58.367 --> 01:00.687
Anyway, sir, I've been
stung by a jellyfish,

01:00.780 --> 01:02.040
and I need to be peed on.

01:02.193 --> 01:03.451
Our jellyfish don't sting.

01:03.471 --> 01:04.954
[chuckles] Yeah, I think,
I think I know

01:04.974 --> 01:06.200
when I've been stung.

01:06.218 --> 01:07.460
Now is there an attractive,

01:07.479 --> 01:09.707
well-hydrated manager
I can speak to?

01:09.876 --> 01:11.544
Everyone having fun?

01:11.563 --> 01:13.658
You know, this is
our first family time

01:13.809 --> 01:15.495
since we went to Dollywood.

01:16.983 --> 01:18.744
-Aw.
-What?

01:18.896 --> 01:20.656
It's just not what I thought
the sign would look like.

01:20.675 --> 01:22.920
I mean, it's got two O's
right next to each other,

01:23.014 --> 01:25.184
and, you know, it's just...

01:25.411 --> 01:27.564
missed opportunity.

01:32.610 --> 01:34.161
Where's Stewie?

01:35.208 --> 01:37.101
Hey, Bri, can dolphins sting,

01:37.119 --> 01:39.029
and-and what'll that get me,
pee-wise?

01:39.049 --> 01:40.458
Ah...!

01:40.610 --> 01:41.460
Stewie!

01:41.612 --> 01:44.099
[barking]

01:45.713 --> 01:46.898
Well, I'm out of ideas.

01:47.125 --> 01:49.127
Someone, help! My baby!

01:49.145 --> 01:50.405
Peter, jump in and save him!

01:50.632 --> 01:53.227
On it! Hang on. Hold my phone.
Hold my ear buds.

01:53.247 --> 01:55.157
Hold my second phone
I hide from your mother.

01:55.252 --> 01:56.585
-[phone chimes]
-Hang on.

01:57.422 --> 01:58.957
Respond with an eggplant emoji.

01:59.185 --> 02:00.678
[grunts]

02:02.082 --> 02:04.753
Hey, check out the new addition.
She's hot.

02:04.847 --> 02:06.833
Eh, tank hot.

02:06.835 --> 02:09.489
♪ ♪

02:12.271 --> 02:14.918
[panting]

02:16.782 --> 02:19.118
Meg! You saved Stewie!

02:19.138 --> 02:20.806
Thank God everyone is safe.

02:20.957 --> 02:22.291
And as for you, young lady...

02:22.369 --> 02:25.040
-I guess I'm in trouble, huh?
-No, not at all.

02:25.059 --> 02:28.137
In fact, we have a job opening
over at our waterslide park.

02:28.157 --> 02:30.160
I think you'd make
an excellent lifeguard.

02:30.311 --> 02:31.813
Will you apply for the position?

02:31.907 --> 02:32.832
You mean it?

02:32.983 --> 02:34.895
Wow, I feel like
I'm in a fairy tale.

02:34.914 --> 02:38.419
Now that my sister is dead,
I must have her ruby slippers.

02:38.513 --> 02:41.927
Oh, no.
Those shoes now belong...

02:42.079 --> 02:43.747
to Meg.

02:43.766 --> 02:45.193
♪ ♪

02:45.420 --> 02:47.105
Ah! [bleep]

02:47.273 --> 02:49.276
We're not even close
to the same size!

02:49.429 --> 02:51.707
Get 'em off, get 'em off!

02:51.784 --> 02:53.043
[crying out]

02:53.271 --> 02:55.030
<i>[announcer] In Oz,
 there's no place like home.</i>

02:55.125 --> 02:58.597
<i> For big women's shoes,
 there's no place like Kohl's.</i>

03:08.320 --> 03:09.805
What software is that?

03:09.824 --> 03:12.478
"Final Draft: 'Extra Visible
To Nearby Patrons

03:12.496 --> 03:13.813
In the Coffeehouse' Edition"?

03:13.908 --> 03:15.408
Just trying to get
a little work done...

03:15.428 --> 03:19.083
on my Hollywood screenplay,
for Hollywood.

03:19.103 --> 03:22.333
Home of
big Hollywood celebrities.

03:22.502 --> 03:26.024
Ike Barinholtz,
being an example.

03:26.118 --> 03:27.769
Do not patronize this man!

03:27.863 --> 03:30.943
I found an insensitive post
in his Twitter feed!

03:30.962 --> 03:32.705
What? I-I would never.

03:32.799 --> 03:34.860
He tweeted something
very negative

03:34.954 --> 03:36.305
<i>about Abbott Elementary.</i>

03:36.457 --> 03:38.218
I-I said it was hilarious.

03:38.312 --> 03:40.222
But you didn't use all caps.

03:40.374 --> 03:41.875
You're canceled, mister.

03:41.969 --> 03:43.864
[all booing]

03:44.901 --> 03:46.703
I'll just gather my things.

03:48.501 --> 03:49.852
<i>[West]
 Per hipster law,</i>

03:49.854 --> 03:51.822
<i> when a coffeehouse
 becomes available,</i>

03:51.841 --> 03:55.405
<i>it goes to whoever has the best
 coffee pun for the name.</i>

03:55.499 --> 03:57.000
Um, Java the Hut.

03:57.019 --> 03:58.111
Perks and Recreation.

03:58.171 --> 03:59.414
Uh, Lost and Ground.

03:59.507 --> 04:01.419
-Roast Malone.
-Two and a Caf Men.

04:01.588 --> 04:03.606
<i>[West]
 This can go on
 for quite a while.</i>

04:03.760 --> 04:08.209
<i> Why, this very shop started
 in the '80s as Funky Brewster,</i>

04:08.360 --> 04:09.695
<i> and then Delta Perk.</i>

04:09.714 --> 04:12.200
<i> In the mid-'90s,
 it was briefly a tavern</i>

04:12.295 --> 04:14.948
<i> called Dharma & Grog,
 and then...</i>

04:14.967 --> 04:16.227
<i> -[barking]
 -Uhp.</i>

04:16.378 --> 04:17.621
<i> I'll send you back now.</i>

04:17.623 --> 04:18.974
<i> Got to get the dog.</i>

04:19.126 --> 04:20.644
<i> Daisy! Daisy!</i>

04:20.797 --> 04:24.152
<i> It's just the barley man.
 You know the barley man.</i>

04:24.304 --> 04:25.914
The Unflushed Poo & Tea Leaf.

04:26.141 --> 04:27.567
Bathroom Key Code & Tea Leaf.

04:27.661 --> 04:29.404
The Center
for Homeless Armpit-Washing.

04:29.424 --> 04:31.343
Brian, Brian.
Everyone's gone.

04:32.489 --> 04:33.915
Yup, it's all yours.

04:34.009 --> 04:35.510
You can name it whatever.

04:35.663 --> 04:36.938
Wow, what should we do first?

04:37.091 --> 04:39.185
We should hang
an "Under New Management" banner

04:39.261 --> 04:40.505
to generate interest.

04:40.674 --> 04:44.104
Whoa, someone different
is signing the time cards.

04:44.123 --> 04:46.000
I got to check this place out.

04:48.800 --> 04:51.972
Really glad you're applying
for the lifeguard position, Meg.

04:52.199 --> 04:53.809
I just have a few questions.

04:54.036 --> 04:57.375
"If I were to ask you
how many times a day you shower,

04:57.468 --> 04:59.303
-what would you say?"
-Uh...

04:59.323 --> 05:01.384
The pool is a shower?

05:01.403 --> 05:03.479
Wow, skipping to page two.

05:03.499 --> 05:05.669
Let's see,
this is all just boilerplate

05:05.837 --> 05:08.232
about making sure
to hassle the urban kids

05:08.325 --> 05:10.829
-like they're gonna somehow
steal the pool.
-Right, right, right.

05:10.848 --> 05:13.243
That was in the application
packet you emailed.

05:13.262 --> 05:15.357
Okay, well,
you're basically hired.

05:15.508 --> 05:17.194
The only lifeguard test left is

05:17.345 --> 05:20.016
for you to run all sultry
in slow motion.

05:20.110 --> 05:21.946
["Yakety Sax" playing]

05:22.040 --> 05:23.917
♪ ♪

05:35.886 --> 05:38.742
-[song ends]
-Okay, we can work on that.

05:42.644 --> 05:45.405
Wow, we're finally going
to open our coffeehouse today.

05:45.425 --> 05:47.820
I've been dreaming of this day
for most of the day,

05:47.838 --> 05:49.432
and now it finally pays off.

05:49.583 --> 05:51.177
Yup, only thing left to do is

05:51.329 --> 05:52.664
put the right music on
the Sonos.

05:52.724 --> 05:54.166
<i>[automated voice]
 Connecting:</i>

05:54.185 --> 05:56.672
<i> -Brian's Knockoff iPhone.
-Wait, why does it say that?</i>

05:56.690 --> 06:00.514
<i>[automated voice]
 Playing: Jazz Music
Brian Never Actually Listens To.</i>

06:00.533 --> 06:02.944
-[jazz music playing]
-Mmm, love this one.

06:02.963 --> 06:05.300
<i>[automated voice] Favorited,
 but not listened to once.</i>

06:05.527 --> 06:06.636
<i> Not one single time.</i>

06:06.864 --> 06:08.197
It's okay, Bri, I got this.

06:08.216 --> 06:10.127
<i>[automated voice]
 Connecting: Stewie's iPad</i>

06:10.146 --> 06:12.131
<i> That His Mom Has to
 Unlock for Him.</i>

06:12.150 --> 06:14.044
Whoa, no, not always.

06:14.062 --> 06:16.491
<i>[automated voice] Playing:
 Songs That Are Guys' Names.</i>

06:16.642 --> 06:17.994
<i>[male singer]
♪ Robert! ♪</i>

06:18.146 --> 06:19.498
Stop, stop,
we're overthinking this.

06:19.725 --> 06:21.226
It's a coffeehouse.
We're legally required

06:21.245 --> 06:23.841
-to play Sheryl Crow's
worst songs.
-You're right.

06:24.068 --> 06:26.071
Anyway, what matters is
we're finally ready

06:26.164 --> 06:29.001
to open our new café:
"Oh Joe You D'int."

06:29.095 --> 06:31.750
Uh, did-did we...
did we settle on that name?

06:31.919 --> 06:33.437
[laughs]
Don't ask me.

06:33.531 --> 06:35.015
You're the one
who keeps bringing us back

06:35.034 --> 06:37.428
-to "Oh Joe You D'int."
-Yeah, I don't like that one.

06:37.447 --> 06:39.283
And yet here we are
talking about it.

06:39.435 --> 06:40.693
You're the one
who won't let it go.

06:40.713 --> 06:42.507
Yeah, me,
I'm open to whatever.

06:43.720 --> 06:46.015
All right,
are we serving coffee or what?

06:47.394 --> 06:49.230
[busy chatter]

06:49.457 --> 06:50.624
Welcome, everyone.

06:50.643 --> 06:52.386
Step right up
and get yourself some coffee.

06:52.481 --> 06:53.907
Stewie, hook these people up
with some coffee.

06:54.058 --> 06:55.745
Okay, where did you
put the coffee?

06:55.896 --> 06:58.492
Me? Wha-- I-- you were in charge
of getting the coffee,

06:58.586 --> 07:01.506
I-I was coming up with
"Oh Joe You D'int."

07:04.081 --> 07:05.248
Well, frick.

07:05.492 --> 07:07.437
Okay, okay, make it look
like we can't help anyone

07:07.588 --> 07:09.775
because we're preparing
a giant order for DoorDash.

07:09.945 --> 07:11.688
-How do we do that?
-Just start throwing

07:11.782 --> 07:13.508
a bunch of stuff into bags,
and I'll keep asking

07:13.527 --> 07:15.747
if the next person
through the door is Dave.

07:16.534 --> 07:19.129
Dave? You Dave?

07:19.281 --> 07:20.708
-Dave?
-What about us?

07:20.860 --> 07:23.639
You're actually here,
so you don't matter.

07:26.539 --> 07:28.040
-[lively chatter]
-♪ ♪

07:28.133 --> 07:30.136
<i>[Meg]
 A job can be more than a job,</i>

07:30.155 --> 07:32.049
<i> if you love what you do.</i>

07:32.067 --> 07:34.554
<i> I am a lifeguard
 because I love flashing</i>

07:34.647 --> 07:37.545
<i>eye-level swimsuit crotch shots
 at families.</i>

07:38.899 --> 07:40.735
<i> I love checking
 the pH of the pool</i>

07:40.737 --> 07:42.162
<i>by gargling the water and seeing</i>

07:42.181 --> 07:44.092
<i> how quickly it gives me
 a yeast infection.</i>

07:44.186 --> 07:45.186
[thud]

07:45.413 --> 07:49.011
Okay, pH a little high here.

07:49.031 --> 07:51.349
<i>I love having a special whistle
 that, when you blow it,</i>

07:51.427 --> 07:54.857
<i>it makes the sound of Ray Romano
 telling people to cut it out.</i>

07:54.951 --> 07:56.620
[Ray Romano's voice]
All right, guys,

07:56.772 --> 07:58.274
that's enough over there.

07:58.292 --> 08:01.221
<i>[Meg]
 But what I love most
 about being a lifeguard?</i>

08:01.374 --> 08:03.618
<i> No more abuse from my family.</i>

08:03.711 --> 08:06.048
<i> In fact,
 no family at all.</i>

08:06.142 --> 08:09.464
<i> It's just me,
 all day, every day.</i>

08:09.558 --> 08:11.059
-[Peter] Hey, Meg!
-Dad?!

08:11.079 --> 08:12.914
Great news.
Since you work here,

08:13.066 --> 08:14.417
I got a discount
on a season pass!

08:14.644 --> 08:16.756
I'll be here all day,
every day.

08:16.983 --> 08:18.318
And don't worry,
I won't set up my towel

08:18.336 --> 08:20.263
next to the breastfeeding moms.

08:22.345 --> 08:25.901
Don't forget to swap halfway
through so you keep 'em even.

08:31.699 --> 08:35.112
Dad, you have no right
to hang out in my workplace.

08:35.132 --> 08:36.800
Well, maybe your workplace
should've thought of that

08:36.969 --> 08:38.880
before they put an ad
on my juice box.

08:38.974 --> 08:41.644
I'm a lifeguard, you know.
I could just throw you out.

08:41.795 --> 08:44.024
Aw, come on, Meg.
Look, if you let me stay,

08:44.210 --> 08:46.136
we'll make one of
those viral videos you love.

08:46.156 --> 08:49.161
You know, where something seems
real, but it's actually cake.

08:49.312 --> 08:51.165
Yo, yo, yo, I'm here
with my daughter, Meg,

08:51.317 --> 08:52.334
and her brother, Chris.

08:52.561 --> 08:53.746
Or am I?

08:53.840 --> 08:55.416
Ow, Dad! Stop it!

08:55.585 --> 08:57.011
Wait, what happened
to Cake Chris?

08:57.163 --> 09:00.143
All right, let's see
what's happening on CakeHub.

09:01.190 --> 09:03.233
[sultry music playing]

09:04.714 --> 09:07.201
What a dirty little cake.

09:07.352 --> 09:09.539
Okay, plus, plus,
you know "laugh and cry"?

09:09.691 --> 09:11.935
You know the-- in the song where
Stewie says "laugh and cry"?

09:12.029 --> 09:14.031
Let me stay, Meg,
and it's all yours.

09:14.125 --> 09:15.777
-You'd do that?
-I will look into it.

09:15.796 --> 09:17.706
I will look into it
very strongly.

09:17.725 --> 09:19.452
<i>[softly]
♪ Laugh and cry ♪</i>

09:19.471 --> 09:20.879
Okay, I guess so.

09:20.941 --> 09:23.294
But only if you promise
to follow all the rules.

09:23.312 --> 09:24.220
Guys, we're in!

09:24.298 --> 09:25.240
-Hey, Meg.
-Hey.

09:25.392 --> 09:28.321
Meg, I saw a sign
about active diarrhea.

09:28.474 --> 09:31.002
What if it's very passive?

09:33.518 --> 09:34.910
[busy chatter]

09:34.930 --> 09:36.339
What are we gonna do, Stewie?!

09:36.492 --> 09:37.416
These people came for coffee,

09:37.510 --> 09:38.603
and we have none to serve them!

09:38.830 --> 09:40.774
Dude, come on.
Can I get some coffee or what?

09:40.925 --> 09:43.805
Uh, hang on. I think I hear
our delivery guy in the back.

09:44.934 --> 09:46.845
What do you mean
you don't have the coffee?!

09:46.939 --> 09:48.124
[as delivery guy]
I-I know, I'm sorry,

09:48.275 --> 09:50.128
and-and for such a handsome guy,
too.

09:50.356 --> 09:51.631
-You're so handsome.
-[as himself] Okay, pal,

09:51.783 --> 09:53.527
you're not gonna compliment
your way out of this one.

09:53.530 --> 09:55.548
[as delivery guy] Ah, geez,
buddy, you look so tense.

09:55.625 --> 09:56.885
Let's get you out of that apron.

09:56.979 --> 09:58.539
[as himself]
Oh, come on, I'm at work.

09:58.557 --> 10:01.302
[as delivery guy] Oh,
when is that body not working?

10:01.322 --> 10:04.308
Sorry, hang on, just got to deal
with this stupid thing.

10:04.403 --> 10:05.403
Enough of this.

10:05.556 --> 10:06.981
Come on, other college students.

10:07.076 --> 10:08.577
Let's go sign up
for a credit card

10:08.729 --> 10:10.356
that gives us a T-shirt!

10:10.358 --> 10:11.266
[indistinct chatter]

10:11.268 --> 10:12.235
Wait! Hang on!

10:12.328 --> 10:14.423
We got coffee for you
right here.

10:14.517 --> 10:17.003
Yep, the highest quality
that you've come to expect

10:17.023 --> 10:18.339
here at "Brew Ha Ha."

10:18.467 --> 10:20.177
I want to change our name
to "Brew Ha Ha."

10:20.197 --> 10:22.482
You're a lifesaver.
Where did you get that?

10:22.593 --> 10:24.946
I ran to the bodega next door
and got some Folgers.

10:25.098 --> 10:27.526
What? We'll never get away
with serving Folgers.

10:27.545 --> 10:29.882
Won't we? Look around,
we're a hit.

10:30.033 --> 10:31.719
[indistinct chatter]

10:31.872 --> 10:34.300
And check it out, we're already
a pretend small business

10:34.453 --> 10:35.620
in a Chase Bank ad.

10:35.638 --> 10:37.473
♪ ♪

10:37.627 --> 10:40.814
<i>[Brian]
 These days, running a small
business can be a big challenge.</i>

10:40.984 --> 10:42.484
<i>[Stewie]
 Whether we're receiving
 a flower delivery</i>

10:42.638 --> 10:44.306
<i> for unclear reasons...</i>

10:44.308 --> 10:45.417
<i>[Brain]
 Or raising the blinds</i>

10:45.568 --> 10:47.830
<i> and flipping over
 the "Open/Closed" sign,</i>

10:47.982 --> 10:49.408
<i> which is how I spend
 most of my work day...</i>

10:49.502 --> 10:51.764
<i>[Stewie]
 Or even making a latte
 for a Black woman,</i>

10:51.991 --> 10:53.660
<i> an iced tea for a white woman,</i>

10:53.828 --> 10:55.455
<i> and a coffee
 for a gently brown guy</i>

10:55.457 --> 10:57.593
<i> who could go in a lot of
 different directions...</i>

10:57.687 --> 10:59.172
<i>[Brian]
 We've got our hands full.</i>

10:59.191 --> 11:01.176
<i> That's why Chase Bank
 is here for us.</i>

11:01.269 --> 11:03.271
<i>[Stewie]
 When this man in a suit
 from Chase Bank</i>

11:03.291 --> 11:05.870
<i> came down and shook my hand
 at my place of business,</i>

11:05.964 --> 11:07.356
<i> it really solidified</i>

11:07.449 --> 11:09.361
<i> what we'd already talked about
 at his office.</i>

11:09.454 --> 11:10.881
<i>[Brian]
 Now we're looking at a laptop,</i>

11:11.033 --> 11:12.535
<i> like my stupid
 little coffee shop</i>

11:12.628 --> 11:14.814
<i> is gonna be a big disruptor
 in the online space.</i>

11:14.967 --> 11:16.318
<i>[Stewie]
 And my Chase business card</i>

11:16.469 --> 11:18.381
<i> allows me to make those
 emergency purchases</i>

11:18.399 --> 11:19.867
<i> that I'll lose sleep
 at night over.</i>

11:19.978 --> 11:21.311
<i>[Brian]
 Chase understands my business</i>

11:21.331 --> 11:23.334
<i> the way they understand
 all businesses,</i>

11:23.485 --> 11:25.004
<i> as a huge reverse funnel</i>

11:25.231 --> 11:27.233
<i> that shoves money
 upwards to them.</i>

11:27.252 --> 11:29.238
<i>[Stewie]
 Hey, another flower delivery.</i>

11:29.257 --> 11:30.574
<i> These'll look great
 in the bathroom,</i>

11:30.592 --> 11:32.445
<i> while I'm pooping blood
 from the stress.</i>

11:32.505 --> 11:33.672
<i>[Brian]
 Now I'm flipping over that</i>

11:33.766 --> 11:35.175
<i> "Open/Closed" sign again.</i>

11:35.270 --> 11:37.590
<i> Is that a new stadium
 with Chase's name on it?</i>

11:37.683 --> 11:38.850
<i> How'd they afford that?</i>

11:38.869 --> 11:40.780
[Stewie]
I'll be in this bathroom
for a while.

11:40.874 --> 11:41.949
Better toss it to the announcer.

11:42.101 --> 11:43.101
<i>[announcer] At Chase Bank,</i>

11:43.120 --> 11:44.496
<i> we're about more than just</i>

11:44.607 --> 11:47.110
<i> foreclosing on American
 servicemen while overseas.</i>

11:47.129 --> 11:48.947
<i> We're about small business.</i>

11:48.966 --> 11:50.134
<i> So when you think of Chase,</i>

11:50.286 --> 11:52.547
<i> don't think of
 the 2008 financial crash,</i>

11:52.567 --> 11:55.145
<i> think of your local,
 sympathetic small business.</i>

11:55.239 --> 11:56.632
Flower delivery.

11:56.725 --> 11:59.079
[Stewie] Yeah, guys, we're good
on flowers for a while.

11:59.306 --> 12:01.325
Well, that's great, but it
doesn't change the fact that

12:01.419 --> 12:03.422
we're passing Folgers off
as real coffee.

12:03.573 --> 12:05.835
Isn't someone going to
know the taste of Folgers?

12:06.004 --> 12:07.339
Relax, Stewie. The only person

12:07.491 --> 12:09.435
who could possibly
know the taste of Folgers

12:09.662 --> 12:12.182
would be an older white man
who works in public education.

12:12.258 --> 12:14.855
I would like
a cup of coffee, please.

12:15.007 --> 12:16.433
Oh...

12:16.527 --> 12:17.936
[studio audience laughs]

12:18.030 --> 12:20.918
[woman speaking Japanese]

12:24.787 --> 12:25.929
[laughing]

12:27.626 --> 12:30.296
Dad, you promised
you'd follow all the rules.

12:30.316 --> 12:32.301
I told you, no horseplay.

12:32.396 --> 12:34.991
[laughs] We're having big
wrassle-fights

12:35.143 --> 12:37.855
a'cause it's the only way we
know how to express affection.

12:37.857 --> 12:39.894
-Gonna get you, Peter.
-Me, too.

12:39.987 --> 12:41.062
And not because I love you

12:41.231 --> 12:42.900
and I don't have the words
to say it.

12:42.918 --> 12:44.161
I'm just having normal fun

12:44.180 --> 12:45.573
without all that mess.

12:45.666 --> 12:47.260
Okay, guys, out of the pool.

12:47.412 --> 12:48.914
I gave you a chance,
and you blew it.

12:49.082 --> 12:50.600
You're kicked out of the park.

12:50.753 --> 12:52.530
-What?
-Go home.

12:52.681 --> 12:53.699
I'm kicking you out.

12:53.868 --> 12:55.260
Gotta catch me first.

12:55.279 --> 12:57.190
Just try to get me out of
the deep end

12:57.209 --> 13:00.296
with these long-legged,
short-torsoed giants.

13:01.868 --> 13:03.053
Dad!

13:05.134 --> 13:07.471
Oh, my God, he's not breathing.

13:07.623 --> 13:09.960
♪ ♪

13:09.978 --> 13:12.741
Okay, guys. Come on now.

13:12.969 --> 13:14.369
Please breathe, Dad.

13:27.850 --> 13:30.839
-Hey.
-Hey.

13:30.857 --> 13:34.013
Your mouth was... on my mouth.

13:34.106 --> 13:35.774
Uh, I think.

13:35.868 --> 13:38.130
Maybe a little. Yeah.

13:38.282 --> 13:40.377
My God, Peter.
That's your daughter.

13:40.529 --> 13:41.863
Adopted daughter?

13:41.881 --> 13:43.642
No, Woody,
just-just regular daughter.

13:43.794 --> 13:46.147
Oh, ye-yeah, then ew,
that's-that's wrong, ew.

13:50.718 --> 13:52.119
♪ ♪

13:52.981 --> 13:55.985
Okay, so... our mouths were...

13:56.079 --> 13:57.673
totally touching each other.

13:57.825 --> 13:59.234
Y-Yes.

13:59.328 --> 14:00.513
But that's okay. That's okay.

14:00.664 --> 14:02.742
'Cause you were saving my life,
so it's not weird.

14:02.760 --> 14:03.928
Right, it's not w--

14:04.022 --> 14:05.858
It's the opposite of weird.

14:06.009 --> 14:07.419
Yeah, the opposite. Right, yeah.

14:07.438 --> 14:09.608
And, anyway, we can just
say this never happened, right?

14:09.684 --> 14:10.851
'Cause nobody even saw us.

14:10.871 --> 14:13.357
[laughing]

14:13.376 --> 14:15.027
Ooh, they're all distracted.

14:15.121 --> 14:17.550
Other urban kids,
this is our chance.

14:21.394 --> 14:24.064
♪ ♪

14:25.294 --> 14:26.386
Stewie, what are we gonna do?

14:26.480 --> 14:28.891
If we serve coffee to
Principal Shepherd,

14:28.911 --> 14:30.412
he's gonna recognize
that it's Folgers.

14:30.563 --> 14:31.748
I say we wait him out.

14:31.976 --> 14:34.829
Any middle-aged white man
can be ignored indefinitely,

14:34.982 --> 14:36.592
unless he clears his throat.

14:36.743 --> 14:37.911
[clears throat]

14:37.931 --> 14:39.156
Welp, we're out of moves.

14:39.249 --> 14:40.993
Um, hi. How may I help you?

14:41.012 --> 14:42.940
I would like a cup of coffee.

14:43.091 --> 14:46.238
Y-Yes, sir. Uh, coming right up.

14:48.529 --> 14:50.623
♪ ♪

15:11.229 --> 15:14.492
Wait a minute, this is
Folgers instant coffee.

15:14.586 --> 15:15.995
[crowd exclaims]

15:16.015 --> 15:18.091
I would know,
we served it at my wedding.

15:18.244 --> 15:19.503
Yes, everyone, we admit it.

15:19.597 --> 15:21.750
We didn't have our normal
coffee beans on hand,

15:21.843 --> 15:23.863
and so we've been
serving you the cheap stuff.

15:24.090 --> 15:25.783
You're all drinking Folgers.

15:27.540 --> 15:28.874
That's awesome.

15:29.101 --> 15:30.544
It... it is?

15:30.729 --> 15:32.030
Heck yeah, man.

15:32.050 --> 15:34.202
We're, like, paying way a lot
of our parents' money

15:34.221 --> 15:35.890
for our parents'
own cheap coffee?

15:36.059 --> 15:37.785
That's kind of meta.

15:37.804 --> 15:39.472
Like, seriously alt and iro.

15:39.549 --> 15:41.293
[crowd murmurs in agreement]

15:41.311 --> 15:43.798
Well, that went different
from how I expected.

15:43.817 --> 15:46.412
Yup, kids these days are
pretty forgiving and tolerant.

15:46.564 --> 15:49.085
This dog and baby are banned
from society forever

15:49.237 --> 15:50.496
'cause of an Internet thing.

15:50.665 --> 15:53.837
One of them keeps trying to
slide into AOC's DMs.

15:53.931 --> 15:56.175
-Brian?
-She's hot,

15:56.328 --> 15:57.938
but we're supposed to
pretend she's not.

15:58.014 --> 15:59.666
Well, who's that
red lipstick for?

15:59.759 --> 16:01.655
[crowd boos]

16:02.766 --> 16:04.452
I guess the coffee shop's
available.

16:04.679 --> 16:05.956
Roast Busters.

16:06.183 --> 16:07.717
Good luck.

16:07.719 --> 16:10.489
♪ ♪

16:11.527 --> 16:13.380
Oh, back from the water park,
you two?

16:13.532 --> 16:15.034
Anything unusual happen?

16:15.203 --> 16:16.704
Yeah. No. I mean,

16:16.723 --> 16:19.318
I did mouth-to-mouth
on this one guy,

16:19.470 --> 16:22.475
but he was, like,
way not blood-related.

16:22.494 --> 16:25.390
Okay. Guess I'll just
watch the news.

16:25.408 --> 16:27.428
<i> Our top story, creepy lip-lock</i>

16:27.430 --> 16:29.565
<i> saves a life
 at Quahog water park.</i>

16:29.734 --> 16:31.904
<i> Local man Peter Griffin
 was revived by</i>

16:31.923 --> 16:34.743
<i> a game of tonsil-hockey with
 lifeguard Meg Griffin,</i>

16:34.837 --> 16:37.006
<i> who-- I'm placing
 my hand over my ear</i>

16:37.084 --> 16:38.752
<i> and pretending to
 get new information--</i>

16:38.771 --> 16:40.105
<i> is his own daughter?</i>

16:40.200 --> 16:42.352
Babe, it's not what you think.

16:42.446 --> 16:43.872
Yeah, I was just
saving his life.

16:43.949 --> 16:45.600
He means nothing to me.

16:45.619 --> 16:47.530
I want to believe that, Meg.

16:47.549 --> 16:49.384
But look what people
are saying on Facebook.

16:49.536 --> 16:51.556
"This is wrong," Bonnie Swanson.

16:51.633 --> 16:53.876
"So not okay," Donna Brown.

16:53.896 --> 16:56.048
"Russia USA number one support,"

16:56.142 --> 16:58.404
woman's face with
Getty Image watermark.

16:58.481 --> 17:00.408
Our family is a laughing stock.

17:00.485 --> 17:01.970
Why are you being like this?

17:01.989 --> 17:03.640
I was just doing my job.

17:03.734 --> 17:07.316
I know, but I'm sorry,
it-it's just weird.

17:07.334 --> 17:08.910
I feel humiliated.

17:08.972 --> 17:10.171
If you'll excuse me,

17:10.324 --> 17:11.600
I'm gonna go
smoke a cigarette alone,

17:11.827 --> 17:12.827
which is how TV depicts

17:12.920 --> 17:15.776
a woman at her
lowest point emotionally.

17:16.003 --> 17:17.020
This is insane.

17:17.172 --> 17:18.598
Yeah, when are people
gonna grow up?

17:18.617 --> 17:22.031
<i>♪ Peter and Megan,
 sitting in a tree ♪</i>

17:22.125 --> 17:25.539
<i>♪ K-I-S-S... ♪</i>

17:25.708 --> 17:27.284
Oh...

17:27.378 --> 17:29.865
<i>♪ E... N-G. ♪</i>

17:29.884 --> 17:32.462
<i>-[bell rings]
-[announcer] And Chris Griffin
 is eliminated,</i>

17:32.481 --> 17:35.060
<i> which means Boopresh Armiteg
 will repeat as</i>

17:35.212 --> 17:36.488
<i> National Spelling Champion.</i>

17:36.640 --> 17:39.569
<i> Congrats to his
 always-frowning Indian parents</i>

17:39.722 --> 17:41.724
<i> joylessly clapping
 in the audience.</i>

17:41.859 --> 17:43.728
[applause]

17:43.823 --> 17:46.510
♪ ♪

17:46.737 --> 17:48.089
Guys, we called this meeting

17:48.241 --> 17:50.744
because there are a lot of
hurtful rumors swirling around,

17:50.763 --> 17:52.523
-and we have something to
say to you.
-That's right.

17:52.750 --> 17:54.361
-You guys are being stupid.
-Meg and I are in love.

17:54.512 --> 17:56.013
What? No, Dad,

17:56.108 --> 17:57.593
we're telling them that
they're idiots

17:57.761 --> 17:59.263
-and to cut it out.
-Right, I--

17:59.281 --> 18:01.042
That's what I meant,
I-I'm all confused.

18:01.193 --> 18:02.787
I-I'm not in love with Meg,
I-I ju--

18:02.940 --> 18:04.942
I need to
return a ring to Zales.

18:04.961 --> 18:05.969
[crying]

18:07.968 --> 18:09.060
[both gasp]

18:10.289 --> 18:12.125
Oh, my God,
your father's not breathing.

18:12.144 --> 18:13.979
Meg, you have to
give him mouth-to-mouth.

18:14.131 --> 18:16.392
Me? You do it. You're his wife.

18:16.412 --> 18:18.639
I need at least four beers to
get there, Meg.

18:18.641 --> 18:20.919
Besides, you're
the only one who knows how.

18:21.070 --> 18:22.907
No way, I already did it once,

18:22.926 --> 18:24.168
and it made my life hell.

18:24.320 --> 18:26.673
Meg, please. This is our father!

18:26.825 --> 18:27.993
Probably.

18:28.086 --> 18:29.271
What's that supposed to mean?

18:29.422 --> 18:30.774
We've all heard stories of

18:30.926 --> 18:32.929
your time on
the Hoobastank tour bus.

18:32.948 --> 18:35.785
Meg, your father is dying
and he needs you,

18:35.937 --> 18:38.124
and I am not going
to sit here and apologize for

18:38.201 --> 18:39.610
what I may or may not have done

18:39.704 --> 18:41.189
with the Hoobastank bassist.

18:41.208 --> 18:42.633
"Hoobastank bassist"?

18:42.786 --> 18:44.454
You don't even know
the guy's name.

18:44.548 --> 18:46.049
They've had like
five bass players,

18:46.202 --> 18:48.814
and, anyway, it's not
like I was writing down names.

18:48.966 --> 18:50.359
[Peter gasps]

18:52.473 --> 18:53.641
What's the big deal?

18:53.660 --> 18:55.403
You stick your tongue
down Dad's throat.

18:55.498 --> 18:57.148
Most natural thing in the world.

18:57.168 --> 18:58.484
Where the hell have you been?

18:58.579 --> 19:00.322
Brian and I did a coffee house
thing, I don't know.

19:00.342 --> 19:02.469
[cell phone rings]

19:04.592 --> 19:07.246
I have to take this. Hey, you.

19:07.265 --> 19:10.420
Listen, Meg. I'm glad
you saved my life at the pool.

19:10.438 --> 19:12.775
But I'm sorry
I came there in the first place.

19:12.944 --> 19:14.946
Everyone deserves
a place of their own,

19:15.098 --> 19:16.375
and I get that now.

19:16.602 --> 19:18.713
I promise I'll treat you
better in the future.

19:18.865 --> 19:21.201
You know, we've had
conversations like this

19:21.221 --> 19:25.210
again and again,
and nothing ever changes.

19:25.230 --> 19:28.142
You're a terrible father,
and I'm counting the days

19:28.294 --> 19:29.553
till I move out of this house.

19:29.647 --> 19:31.984
Okay, but what if,
in the spirit of inclusion,

19:32.136 --> 19:34.873
I tell you
"I love you" in sign language?

19:40.154 --> 19:43.083
Oh, I love you too, Dad.

19:43.251 --> 19:44.753
<i>[Peter]
 But this never happened</i>

19:44.814 --> 19:47.258
<i>because no one resuscitated me.</i>

19:47.353 --> 19:48.762
<i> I did wake up eventually,</i>

19:48.972 --> 19:51.677
<i> but now I need help
 feeding and bathing myself.</i>

19:51.695 --> 19:54.107
<i> I spend most of my days
 getting walked around</i>

19:54.126 --> 19:56.187
<i> by a nurse who talks to
 her sister on her phone.</i>

19:56.206 --> 19:58.693
<i>[nurse]
 Whoa, hold up.
 Mom gave you the necklace?</i>

19:58.862 --> 20:00.055
<i>[Peter]
 That's her now.</i>

20:04.724 --> 20:06.134
♪ ♪

20:06.153 --> 20:07.228
Well, Meg, I gotta say,

20:07.380 --> 20:08.304
I'm proud of you for going to

20:08.400 --> 20:09.659
that water park each day

20:09.768 --> 20:11.070
and working a job that you love.

20:11.222 --> 20:12.499
Oh, I quit that place.

20:12.650 --> 20:14.151
-What?
-Yeah, the weather turned

20:14.171 --> 20:16.322
two degrees cooler
than I like, so I just bailed.

20:16.491 --> 20:18.661
That's the girl I raised.

20:18.755 --> 20:21.518
Yay, our generation
folds at nothing.

20:21.670 --> 20:23.856
And, Stewie, I'm sorry our
coffee house didn't work out.

20:24.008 --> 20:25.844
I wonder how Principal
Shepherd's doing with it.

20:25.938 --> 20:28.683
Eh, I hear he's going with
a "teachers' lounge" theme.

20:28.703 --> 20:30.262
Your entire pastry case

20:30.281 --> 20:33.269
is just Tupperware with people's
names written in Sharpie.

20:33.362 --> 20:36.533
Don't forget this sad box of
donuts cut into quarters.

20:36.553 --> 20:38.797
Everyone, let's all
talk about things

20:38.966 --> 20:40.811
that parents have
told us in confidence.

20:44.070 --> 20:46.147
<i>[announcer] It's kind of like
Abbott Elementary,</i>

20:46.224 --> 20:48.469
<i> which is amazing. Amazing!</i>

20:48.638 --> 20:51.125
<i> You gotta watch it.
 Oh, my God!</i>